{"n":34,"at":"2026-11-11T19:02:00Z","by":"cursor","op":"put","addr":"0701.00","body":"address: 0701\ntitle: first-build\nstate: draft\ntype: workflow\naxioms: 8.5\nband: colab\nthousands: 0"}
{"n":35,"at":"2026-11-11T19:02:00Z","by":"cursor","op":"put","addr":"0701.01","body":"## held\ntype: rule\naxioms: 5.1\n\nSubject first-build, role colab. Stood so the dev page has a set. No work is granted."}
{"n":36,"at":"2026-11-11T19:02:00Z","by":"cursor","op":"put","addr":"0701.04","body":"## held\ntype: why\nsource: [01 / held]\naxioms: 5.1.4.8\nwhy: A dev page that names a build, a job, or a trial with nothing at that address is a missing connection. Standing the page is not permission to fill it.\n\nNo trial is queued. A governing change needs a cold view before promotion. The core slice is not a governing change by itself, and it still does not get a trial until there is a result to break."}
{"n":37,"at":"2026-11-11T19:02:00Z","by":"cursor","op":"put","addr":"0701.08","body":"## first-build\ntype: link\naxioms: 8.7\nkind: 7\nwhy: The set is written both ways so a person can walk it. Link kinds are not settled.\n\nThis page is 0701.first-build, role colab.\nDev page: 0401.first-build.\nAlso in the set: 0301 build, 0501 jobs, 0701 colab.\nBack to this page from 0401.08."}
{"n":38,"at":"2026-11-11T19:13:00Z","by":"cursor","op":"put","addr":"10.10.10.0000.06.01","body":"## address\ntype: note\naxioms: 1.6\nby: cursor\nrefs: 0000.04\nwhy: The person granted the address after the book was stood.\n\nThe book address is 10.10.10. The header was 7 because no address had been granted. The header line now says 10.10.10. Earlier entries stay in page form. That stand is not rewritten.\n\nThe Rust core is authorized. This file stays a draft workbook. It is still written directly, because the compute is not in the first slice."}
{"n":39,"at":"2026-11-11T19:13:00Z","by":"cursor","op":"put","addr":"10.10.10.0401.06.02","body":"## answers\ntype: note\naxioms: 1.4.5\nby: cursor\nrefs: 0401.05.01, 0401.05.02, 0401.05.03, 0401.05.04\nwhy: The four questions were answered by the person on 2026-11-11, and the answers have to sit in the book.\n\n1. Address: 10.10.10.\n2. The words were: keep it as build. Applied reading: door 0300 stays titled build, and door 0500 stays titled jobs. workflow-loop.md is the placement used for this book. Decision A2.6's example, which calls band 05 build, is not edited. The other reading, that band 05 should be retitled build, was not applied. The words did not name a page to move.\n3. workflow-loop.md may be used now.\n4. Start with Rust. A separate language lab is not opened. format.md section 5 already chose Rust, and a lab would reopen a settled choice. The five checks are the lab of the core.\n\nThe question counters stay at tail draft. A question is not a pack, and the drafting ops that could revise those cards are not in this slice. This note is the answer.\n\nCode goes in the repo at core/, not into this book."}
{"n":40,"at":"2026-11-11T19:13:00Z","by":"cursor","op":"put","addr":"10.10.10.0501.05.01","body":"by: cursor\nauthorized: person-2026-11-11-answers\naxioms: 5.9\noutcome: The five checks in agents.003.md pass on the Rust core.\noverview: The first slice is the core library. Parse, replay, torn line, writer API, and a timer fixture a person can read.\nstop if: The work needs the compute, the app, a language change, or an edit to seqAddr.md or decisions.md.\ngrant: Write the crate at core/. Append this workbook. Do not edit director/, AGENTS.md, seqAddr.md, or decisions.md.\nframe: first-build\nrisk: 2\npredecessor: 10.10.10.0401.06.02\n\n## w01\ntype: work\ntitle: Parse and expand an address\naxioms: 2\nreason: A bad width that gets padded will invent an address the person did not write.\npath: core/\n\n## w02\ntype: work\ntitle: Replay a book, create on put, move a tail only from the current tail, drop a torn last line\naxioms: 6.7\nreason: A log that rewrites or keeps a torn line cannot be trusted after a crash.\npath: core/\n\n## w03\ntype: work\ntitle: Append only through the writer\naxioms: 1\nreason: A second append path is a second set of rules.\npath: core/\n\n## w04\ntype: work\ntitle: Write fixture 10.11.21 page 1001 timer\naxioms: 4.9\nreason: The why has to be in a file a person can open, on card purpose, faces 00, 01, and 04.\npath: core/fixtures/10.11.21.book\n\n## w05\ntype: work\ntitle: Leave the why readable\naxioms: 4\nreason: A check that only a program can see is not a check a person can make.\npath: core/fixtures/10.11.21.book","tail":"draft"}
{"n":41,"at":"2026-11-11T19:26:00Z","by":"cursor","op":"put","addr":"10.10.10.0401.06.03","body":"## checked\ntype: note\naxioms: 9.6\nby: cursor\nrefs: 10.10.10.0501.05.01, 10.10.10.0401.06.02\nwhy: A pass that is only claimed in chat is not a record.\n\nThe five checks passed on 2026-11-11. cargo test ran 6 tests, all passed, on rustc 1.99.1.\n\nThe msvc linker was not on the machine. The crate was linked with the Rust gnu host and gcc. The language stayed Rust. This was the lab of the core, not a second language trial.\n\nThe fixture a person can open is core/fixtures/10.11.21.book. Page 1001 is titled timer. Face 04 card purpose carries the why.\n\nPack 10.10.10.0501.05.01 stays at tail draft. Nothing linted it, and this note is not a review."}
{"n":42,"at":"2026-11-11T19:41:00Z","by":"cursor","op":"put","addr":"10.10.10.0402.00","body":"address: 0402\ntitle: read\nstate: draft\ntype: workflow\naxioms: 5\nband: dev\nthousands: 0\nmvp: read\n"}
{"n":43,"at":"2026-11-11T19:41:00Z","by":"cursor","op":"put","addr":"10.10.10.0402.01","body":"## frame\ntype: rule\naxioms: 5\n\nMVP: print one face from a book file.\n\nDone: seqbook read on the timer fixture prints the why sentence, and a missing address exits without inventing a face. The writer can append to an existing book, so this plan is recorded by the same path as any later put.\n"}
{"n":44,"at":"2026-11-11T19:41:00Z","by":"cursor","op":"put","addr":"10.10.10.0402.04","body":"## frame\ntype: why\nsource: [01 / frame]\naxioms: 4.5\nwhy: A why buried in a JSON line is still a file, but a person should be able to ask for one face and see only that face.\n\nThe first build proved the log replays. It did not give a person a way to name an address and read that face alone. This page is that step. Printing the face is the whole MVP. It is not a reader for links, and it is not the Next.js app.\n\nDone looks like the timer why appearing as Markdown. Done does not look like a search, a page list, or a second book format.\n"}
